<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#roman-dutch-law">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman-Dutch law</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> law is the legal system that developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in the sixteenth to eighteenth centuries by combining Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> with Dutch custom, and was described by jurists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius">Hugo Grotius</a>. Carried to the Cape by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, it was kept by the British and remains the basis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and several neighbouring countries.</p></div>
